{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016i2GobpgkLRHqqg8aV12ze","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what needs to be built next.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a Go project that re-implements Unix utilities from specifications derived from C source code. The pipeline is: extract specs from C → write PRDs → generate Go code → verify via differential testing against Homebrew GNU binaries.\n\n## Step 2: Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in edge cases. This project derives specs from C source and uses AI code generation to produce Go implementations verified by differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Per-utility Go implementations (ts, wc, cat, sponge, ls, du)\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (terminal width, file metadata, signals)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specs, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current Implementation State**: The testutils and ts packages are implemented, wc is partially done, and the remaining utilities (cat, sponge, ls, du) still need their implementations.\n\n**Roadmap Status**: The ts command is in progress with its first use case, while wc, cat, and sponge are queued for the next release, followed by the ls and du commands in later phases.\n\nLooking at the actual source code, I see implementations already exist for ts, wc, cat, and sponge with their test files. There's a discrepancy here — the implementation status marks ts as pending, but the source code clearly shows main.go and ts_test.go are already present on the current branch. The git status is clean, so these files are committed and real. I need to reconcile this: either the status document is outdated, or these implementations need verification against their specs. all three have implementations and tests already. So rel01.1 is also likely functionally complete, which means the documentation is lagging behind the actual code.\n\nThe next release needing new work is rel02.0, which requires pkg/sys and pkg/format—both still missing implementations. Following the dependency ordering principle, I should build these libraries first before tackling cmd/ls and cmd/du.\n\nSince I can only create one task, pkg/sys is the clear priority as the foundational library both commands depend on. Looking at the requirements, it needs terminal width detection, extended file metadata handling, and signal handlers for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH—all fitting within the 250-350 line target across 5-7 files. Split implementation and tests.\n\nPer P6 (Requirement-anchored decomposition): Anchor to specific PRD requirements.\n\npkg/sys has 4 requirement groups (R1-R4). Let me estimate the size:\n- R1 (Terminal width): ~30-40 lines\n- R2 (Extended file metadata): ~100-150 lines (Stat, Lstat, FileInfo struct, platform-specific code)\n- R3 (SIGPIPE): ~20-30 lines\n- R4 (SIGWINCH): ~30-40 lines\n\nTotal: ~180-260 lines of production code. This fits within one task. But it needs platform-specific files (darwin, linux) so it might be 4-5 files.\n\nFiles needed:\n- pkg/sys/terminal.go (R1: TerminalWidth)\n- pkg/sys/fileinfo.go (R2: FileInfo struct, Stat, Lstat)\n- pkg/sys/fileinfo_darwin.go (R2: platform-specific stat field extraction)\n- pkg/sys/fileinfo_linux.go (R2: platform-specific stat field extraction)\n- pkg/sys/signal.go (R3: SIGPIPE, R4: SIGWINCH)\n\nThat's 5 files, ~250 lines. Fits within constraints.\n\nPer P7 (File naming): The architecture calls the component pkg/sys, so files are in pkg/sys/. Name files after primary exported types/functions.\n\nActually, wait. The prd002-sys acceptance criteria mention compiling on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 (AC5). The platform-specific stat field access is the key divergence point, so I need to make sure the abstraction cleanly separates the platform-dependent logic.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the actual implementation structure: I'll have a common FileInfo interface with Stat and Lstat functions, then platform-specific files that handle the stat struct field extraction differently since Darwin uses st_mtimespec while Linux uses st_mtim. Terminal operations and signal handlers go in their own files since they're independent concerns. This keeps everything under 300 lines and makes the platform differences explicit.\n\nI need to be careful about the task constraints though—the requirement says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, but I can only create one task here. So I'll focus on just the implementation with types included, and defer tests for later.\n\nLet me finalize the requirements list: terminal width detection via system calls, terminal status checking, file metadata structures with platform-specific fields, stat functions, and signal handling for both SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. That gives me seven solid requirements to work with.\n\nFor the design approach, I'm using Go with platform-specific build tags to handle Darwin and Linux differences in stat field extraction, leveraging the unix package for terminal ioctl operations. The SIGPIPE handler will exit cleanly rather than ignore the signal, and terminal resize callbacks will be managed through a mutex-protected slice.\n\nI've outlined seven acceptance criteria covering the return types, field mappings, compilation requirements, and the no-cgo constraint. Now I'm planning the file structure with separate modules for terminal operations, the FileInfo wrapper, and platform-specific stat implementations.\n\nI need to review the product requirements document and architecture guide to ensure my design aligns with the existing constraints, particularly around the golang.org/x/sys dependency and how pkg/sys integrates with the format module that depends on IsTerminal.\n\nLooking at the PRD for OnTerminalResize, it specifies that multiple callbacks must be supported and invoked on resize events, but it doesn't mandate a specific synchronization mechanism. I should describe the design in terms of what the PRD requires—thread-safe callback registration and invocation from signal handlers—rather than prescribing implementation details like sync.Mutex that aren't explicitly derived from the requirements.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the specific design decisions across the system calls and signal handling. I need to ground each one in the PRD: using golang.org/x/sys/unix for terminal size queries rather than shelling out, leveraging build tags to handle platform differences in file metadata structures, ensuring SIGPIPE handlers exit cleanly rather than ignoring the signal, and aligning the handler pattern with what's already established in the codebase. must provide a reusable function that replaces per-utility inline SIGPIPE handling. D4 is valid context since cmd/cat and cmd/sponge already have inline SIGPIPE handling, and pkg/sys provides a shared version that the stitch agent should understand. The FileInfo struct needs all the fields specified in prd002-sys R2.2.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the concrete design choices from prd002-sys: the code must use golang.org/x/sys/unix without shelling out to tput or stty, provide Stat and Lstat functions returning FileInfo with Mode, Size, Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Dev, Ino, Blocks, Blksize, ModTime, and the underlying os.FileInfo, plus additional requirements starting with R3.1. Writing the file structure...\n\nI'm laying out the platform-specific implementations for stat field extraction on Linux and Darwin, along with signal handling utilities for SIGPIPE and terminal resize events. The requirements specify implementing TerminalWidth to fetch column count via ioctl, IsTerminal to check if a file descriptor points to a terminal, and signal handlers to manage terminal-related events gracefully. I'm implementing the Stat and Lstat functions to extract extended file metadata from the underlying syscall structures, then handling platform differences between Darwin and Linux for timestamp and block fields using build tags. I also need to add a SIGPIPE handler that exits cleanly and a terminal resize callback system.\n\nFor the terminal resize handler, I'm registering a SIGWINCH signal handler that queries the current terminal width and invokes all registered callbacks with the new dimensions, using golang.org/x/sys/unix for the ioctl call rather than shelling out to external tools. The design uses build-tagged files to manage platform-specific differences in how stat fields are named and interpreted across Darwin and Linux. The golang.org/x/sys/unix package handles all syscall operations without requiring cgo. Now I'm laying out the acceptance criteria: TerminalWidth needs to return an error when stdout isn't a terminal, Stat and Lstat must populate all the standard file metadata fields correctly, and Lstat specifically needs to show symlink mode bits rather than following to the target.\n\nInstallSIGPIPEHandler sets up a goroutine to catch SIGPIPE and exit cleanly, while OnTerminalResize lets me register multiple callbacks that fire whenever SIGWINCH signals a terminal resize with the new width. The whole pkg/sys codebase needs to compile cleanly on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 without cgo, and pass golangci-lint with no warnings.\n\nNow I'm checking this against the project constraints—task count, line estimates, file count, requirements and acceptance criteria all look aligned with the PRD. But I'm noticing a discrepancy: rel01.0-uc001-ts shows as in_progress, yet the source code already has a ts implementation, so I need to clarify whether that's actually complete or still being worked on.\n\nLooking at the roadmap and spec status more carefully, the specs say \"implementation pending\" for ts, but the code is already there across ts, wc, cat, sponge, and the test utilities. This suggests the roadmap and specs are lagging behind the actual implementation state. Given that rel01.0 and rel01.1 appear to have working code, the next priority should be rel02.0, which requires building out pkg/sys and pkg/format—that's where the earliest incomplete work sits.\n\nFollowing the dependency ordering principle (libraries before CLI tools), pkg/sys needs to come before the command implementations. So implementing pkg/sys is the right move. Let me size this task more precisely by breaking down the components: terminal.go handles width detection and terminal checks with about 30 lines total, fileinfo.go wraps file metadata with the struct definition and stat functions totaling around 90 lines, and the platform-specific stat files handle field extraction for each OS—darwin needs about 30 lines, and linux will need similar handling.\n\nSignal handling rounds things out with SIGPIPE installation and terminal resize callbacks, bringing that module to roughly 55 lines. Adding it all up, I'm looking at around 235 lines, which sits comfortably in the 250-350 range when accounting for documentation and error handling. This is definitely one cohesive task.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":123072,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":123072},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ad748e0-6dd7-472e-9537-1f51e5854752","uuid":"a859a5fd-7ee8-4917-b6c5-18a5ab12ac17"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016i2GobpgkLRHqqg8aV12ze","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities have no formal specifications accessible to implementers. This project derives formal specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI code generation, and verifies parity against Homebrew GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with shared components:\n- `cmd/` — per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness (done)\n- `pkg/sys/` — syscall abstractions for terminal, file metadata, signals (spec complete, not yet implemented)\n- `pkg/format/` — output formatting: columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes (spec complete, not yet implemented)\n- `magefiles/` — build orchestration\n\n**Current State**: Source code exists for `pkg/testutils`, `cmd/ts`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, and `cmd/sponge` (covering rel01.0 and rel01.1). No source code exists yet for `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/ls`, or `cmd/du`.\n\n**Current Release**: rel01.0 and rel01.1 have implementations in the codebase. The next work is rel02.0 (ls core, du), which is blocked on `pkg/sys` and `pkg/format` — neither has been implemented yet.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nrel02.0 requires both `pkg/sys` and `pkg/format` as shared libraries before `cmd/ls` or `cmd/du` can be built. Per P5 (libraries before consumers), `pkg/sys` is the highest priority because `pkg/format` itself depends on `pkg/sys.IsTerminal` (prd003-format R2.3). The dependency chain is: **pkg/sys → pkg/format → cmd/ls, cmd/du**.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"pkg/sys: implement terminal width, extended file metadata, and signal handling (prd002-sys R1-R4)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml (full PRD for pkg/sys -- all requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys component description and technology choices)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml (R2.3 references pkg/sys.IsTerminal for ColorEnabled)\n      - go.mod (verify golang.org/x/sys dependency is declared as the only external dependency)\n      - cmd/cat/main.go (existing inline SIGPIPE handling pattern at lines 49-54 that pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler will replace)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/terminal.go\n        action: create\n        note: TerminalWidth and IsTerminal functions using golang.org/x/sys/unix\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo.go\n        action: create\n        note: FileInfo struct definition, Stat and Lstat wrapper functions\n      - path: pkg/sys/stat_darwin.go\n        action: create\n        note: Darwin-specific stat field extraction (st_mtimespec, st_blocks interpretation)\n      - path: pkg/sys/stat_linux.go\n        action: create\n        note: Linux-specific stat field extraction (st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation)\n      - path: pkg/sys/signal.go\n        action: create\n        note: InstallSIGPIPEHandler and OnTerminalResize functions\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout using golang.org/x/sys/unix. Must return an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails. Per prd002-sys R1.1-R1.2.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file descriptor refers to a terminal. Required by pkg/format (prd003-format R2.3) for ColorEnabled. Use golang.org/x/sys/unix for the check; must not import golang.org/x/term. Per prd002-sys R1.3.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Define a FileInfo struct containing all fields from prd002-sys R2.2: Mode (os.FileMode), Size (int64), Nlink (uint64), Uid (uint32), Gid (uint32), Rdev (uint64), Dev (uint64), Ino (uint64), Blocks (int64), Blksize (int64), ModTime (time.Time), and the underlying os.FileInfo for compatibility with os package functions.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) that call os.Stat/os.Lstat and extract extended fields from the underlying syscall.Stat_t via a platform-specific extraction function. Per prd002-sys R2.1.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in syscall.Stat_t field names using build-tagged source files: st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux, and platform-specific st_blocks interpretation. Per prd002-sys R2.3.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a signal handler calling os.Exit(0) on SIGPIPE. Must not use signal.Ignore; must install a handler so deferred cleanup functions do not run, matching GNU behavior. Per prd002-sys R3.1-R3.2.\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Per prd002-sys R4.1-R4.2.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use golang.org/x/sys/unix for TIOCGWINSZ ioctl. Must not shell out to tput or stty. Must not import golang.org/x/term. Per prd002-sys R1.3.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use Go build tags (//go:build darwin and //go:build linux) for platform-specific stat field extraction. fileinfo.go defines the FileInfo struct and Stat/Lstat functions which call a platform-specific extractStatFields function defined in stat_darwin.go and stat_linux.go. Per prd002-sys R2.3.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"InstallSIGPIPEHandler must install a signal handler that calls os.Exit(0) on SIGPIPE so deferred cleanup functions do not run, matching GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently. Must not use signal.Ignore. Per prd002-sys R3.2.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Lstat on a symlink must return the symlink's own st_mode with type bits showing symlink, not the target's mode. Per prd002-sys AC3.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"No cgo dependency. All syscall access uses golang.org/x/sys/unix which provides pure-Go implementations. Must compile on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no cgo. Per prd002-sys AC5.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"TerminalWidth() compiles and returns (int, error). When stdout is not a terminal, it returns a non-nil error. Per prd002-sys AC1.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Stat() and Lstat() return *FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, Blocks, Blksize, Rdev, Mode, Size, and ModTime fields populated from syscall.Stat_t. Per prd002-sys AC2.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Lstat() on a symlink returns FileInfo with Mode type bits showing symlink, not the target's mode. Per prd002-sys AC3.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"InstallSIGPIPEHandler() registers a handler that calls os.Exit(0) when SIGPIPE is received. Per prd002-sys AC4.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"OnTerminalResize registers callbacks that are invoked with the new terminal width when SIGWINCH is received. Multiple callbacks can be registered via successive calls. Per prd002-sys R4.1-R4.2.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo. Per prd002-sys AC5.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint. Per prd002-sys AC6.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":123072,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":123072},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ad748e0-6dd7-472e-9537-1f51e5854752","uuid":"77762209-7de9-4341-ad0b-cdfa39dd0488"}
